ow German Feudel, Feul, Feil, either from or cognate with Dutch feil (“floorcloth”, attested 1616). Further origin uncertain. Ripuarian Fuddel (“old rag”), if related, would suggest that the d is original, whereas all of the following theories require that it is due to hypercorrection:\n* From Old French faille (“headscarf, veil”), but this is itself of unknown origin and the sense is only a vague match.\n* From Proto-West Germanic *fāgil(u), *fegil(u), a hypothetical tool noun of *fāgōn, *fegōn (“to clean, brush”), whence Dutch vagen, German fegen.\n* From corruption of Proto-West Germanic *þwahilu, tool noun of *þwahan (“to wash”). Compare Dutch dweil, Middle Low German dweile (“cleaning cloth, floorcloth”). This would be the easiest explanation as Dutch dweil and feil are entirely synonymous. A development þw → f is also phonetically plausible, though there seem to be no analogous examples.",{"g":"m","decl":"|||s|||n|"}]],"senses":[[417776,"feudel","noun","a cloth for cleaning, especially a floorcloth",0,"m",["Northern-Germany"]]],"inflections":[["feudel","feudeln","first-person,indicative,present,singular"]]},"gantzer":{"entries":[["gantzer",null,null,null]],"senses":[[383298,"gantzer","adj","inflection of gantz:",0],[383299,"gantzer","adj","strong/mixed nominative masculine singular",1],[383300,"gantzer","adj","strong genitive/dative feminine singular",2],[383301,"gantzer","adj","strong genitive plural",3]],"inflections":[["gantzer","gantz","masculine,nominative,singular,strong"]]},"gedolmetscht":{"entries":[["gedolmetscht",null,null,null]],"senses":[[140186,"gedolmetscht","verb","past participle of dolmetschen",0]],"inflections":[["gedolmetscht","dolmetschen","participle,past"]]},"gegenzügen":{"entries":[["gegenzügen",null,null,null]],"senses":[[349646,"gegenzügen","noun","dative plural of Gegenzug",0,"m"]],"inflections":[["gegenzügen","gegenzug","dative,definite,plural"]]},"glimmer":{"entries":[["glimmer",null,"/ˈɡlɪmɐ/","From glimmen + -er.",{"g":"m","decl":